WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.922 --> 00:00:13.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:19.890
This is the fossilized egg of a giant dinosaur, a titanosaur.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.290
When freshly laid, it would have weighed

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.860
around a kilo and a half, and it's shell

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.005
is about 2 millimeters thick.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.120
While the eggs certainly were tough,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.510
they still needed to be kept safe and warm.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.805
So how did dinosaurs take care of their eggs?

00:00:48.035 --> 00:00:49.910
In order to protect their eggs from predators

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.310
and to keep their eggs warm, dinosaurs

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.650
evolved a list of strategies.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:56.840
One of these strategies was to build a nest

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.440
and then to actually sit on top of the nest.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.450
We know certain dinosaurs did this

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.450
because we actually have fossils of dinosaurs

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.445
sat on top of nests.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.580
Eggs found in these nests contain tiny babies

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.060
of the same species as the adults,

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.990
providing the first evidence that some dinosaurs looked

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:21.995
after their young.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.130
But although this offered protection and shelter

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.040
from the elements, there was a downside

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.270
to incubating eggs in this way.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:36.950
Having to sit on an egg and actually

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.560
look after it means that you are committed

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.890
to the care of that egg for the entire duration

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.330
of their development up until the point of hatching.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.375
For some dinosaurs, it was a commitment worth making.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:02:01.090
But others, like sauropods, faced a different challenge.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.570
It's likely that some dinosaurs never sat on their eggs.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.210
After all, many of these animals weighed tons and tons

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:07.680
and would simply have crushed them

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.345
before they had any chance of hatching.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.725
So what was their solution?

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.290
There are some sauropod egg clutches

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:24.475
where the female excavated a long trench with her back feet.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:33.110
She then backfilled the trench once she had laid all the eggs.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:36.860
This behavior can be seen today.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:41.510
Turtles bury their eggs to protect them from predators,

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:48.390
and the sun warmed sand keeps them at the ideal temperature.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:53.920
But some dinosaurs had another trick to keep their eggs warm.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.770
We think some dinosaur groups deliberately

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.670
collected and piled up rotting vegetation.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.500
They basically formed a compost heap

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.795
on top of their egg filled nests.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.310
Bush turkeys in Australia use this remarkable technique.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.620
As the vegetation breaks down, it

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.860
releases enough heat to incubate their eggs for around seven

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:19.360
weeks.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:25.710
But it was a discovery in 2010 that

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:30.480
revealed that some dinosaurs had an even more unusual way

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.800
of keeping their eggs warm.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.370
They used heat from the Earth itself.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.260
In one particular site in Argentina

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.600
that's had lots of sauropod egg discoveries,

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.080
it's right next door to some geothermal springs.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.730
We're thinking the sauropods were

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:51.030
using that volcanic activity to help keep its eggs warm.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.110
Another location, the Deccan region

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:58.990
of India, one of the most volcanic areas

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:04.420
during the late cretaceous, provides more evidence.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:07.780
There are many, many layers of lava.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.320
Then, in between the lava flows, we find dinosaur eggs.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.589
Dinosaurs were coming to this location

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:19.899
even when it was highly active volcanically,

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:24.160
using it as a nesting site for literally centuries.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.240
Dinosaurs had many ways to ensure their eggs would hatch,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.340
but we can be certain of one thing--

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:44.210
their methods worked very effectively,

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:50.900
helping them to rule the world for over 150 million years.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

